{
  "dom_id": "step-q84847",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-2)^{2}=16(x+6)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+6=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p(x+6) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+6=(4){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t.\n$$",
